1. The Concept of an API (Application Programming Interface)
Websites are built for human eyes—they have pictures, ads, and formatting. Code cannot read that easily. Instead, we use an API. Think of an API like a restaurant menu for computers. We send a highly specific request to a URL (providing our latitude and longitude), and the server sends back raw data without any of the visual fluff. We are using Open-Meteo, a professional open-source API that doesn't require us to create an account, bypass firewalls, or use a paid API key.

2. JSON (JavaScript Object Notation)
When the weather server replies, it sends the data back in a format called JSON. JSON looks like a massive wall of text, but it is structured exactly like a Python Dictionary (with keys and values).
The built-in Python line json.loads(raw_data) is crucial. It translates that raw string of text into a native Python object, allowing us to easily dig in and grab exactly what we want using weather_data['current']['temperature_2m'].

3. WMO Weather Codes
Weather APIs rarely send English words like "Raining" because they serve a global audience. Instead, Open-Meteo sends standard WMO (World Meteorological Organization) numeric codes. Code 0 is clear skies, 95 is a thunderstorm. Our Python script acts as the translator, converting those rigid numbers into the readable English we want to draw on the e-paper screen.